GUI modernization, Phase 5 (third slice): Diagnostics' secret-free
support-summary copy button, with a live preview - already-documented
product spec (`docs/desktop.md`: "what to copy into a support request")
that had never actually been built.

## 1. The gap

`docs/desktop.md`'s own Diagnostics description already promised this;
`Diagnostics.tsx` only ever displayed the information (source, endpoint,
totals, by-family/by-severity breakdowns) with no way to get it into a
support request except retyping it by hand.

## 2. What was built

`buildSupportSummary` (new, exported for direct testing): a pure
function assembling a plain-text summary from exactly the fields already
on screen - app version, source kind, endpoint or bundle file name, last
refresh, event/incident totals, the two breakdowns - using the same
`dict[lang]` labels the page itself renders with, so the copied text
reads in whichever language was on screen when it was copied. Explicitly
secret-free: a bundle path is reduced to its file name only
(`bundlePath.split(/[/\\]/).pop()`), since the full path can contain a
local username or folder structure the way an endpoint (a local pipe/
socket name) does not.

UI: a new card with a read-only, monospace `<textarea>` showing the
live-computed preview (always current, no separate "generate" step -
the "preview before copying" requirement is met by showing it plainly,
not hiding it behind a click) and a "Copy summary" button using
`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, with success/failure feedback that
clears after 2.5s (matching the existing `settings_saved`/
`exportMessage` feedback pattern already used elsewhere in this app).

## 3. Full regression

    $ npx tsc --noEmit                             clean
    $ npm test                                     13 files, 79 tests passed
    $ npm run build                                clean
    $ npx playwright test                          35 passed (0 failed) -
        Diagnostics is one of the 8 pages the untagged-Latin-sentence
        scanner already covers; zero new violations

One real slip caught mid-work, not left in: adding the second batch of
component tests (clipboard mocking) introduced a TypeScript error
(`vi.fn(async () => {})`'s inferred zero-argument mock call tuple made
`.mock.calls[0][0]` an out-of-bounds tuple index) that `npm test`
(vitest, which does not type-check) did not catch but `npm run build`
(which runs `tsc` first) did - a reminder that `tsc --noEmit` needs
re-running after every edit, not just once per slice, since vitest alone
will happily run code with a real type error in it. Fixed by giving the
mock an explicit parameter type.

## 4. Proven secret-free, not just asserted

Six `buildSupportSummary` unit tests, including two specifically for the
redaction claim: a Windows path
(`C:\Users\jsmith\Desktop\incident-42.tar.gz`) and a POSIX path
(`/home/jsmith/incident-42.tar.gz`) each assert the username and folder
segments are absent from the output and only the file name
(`incident-42.tar.gz`) survives - not merely that *some* reduction
happened. A live-endpoint test confirms that value is included as-is
(a local pipe/socket name is not a secret), and a demo-mode test
confirms the endpoint line is omitted entirely rather than shown empty.

Two component tests mock `navigator.clipboard.writeText` to prove both
outcomes: a successful copy shows "Copied" and receives exactly the
preview textarea's own current value (not a stale or reconstructed
copy), and a rejected write shows "Could not copy" rather than an
unhandled promise rejection or a crash.

## 5. Actually looked at it - including an unplanned real-environment proof of the failure path

Fresh browser tab, demo recording. Arabic: the new "ملخص للدعم الفني"
card renders with the correct translated body text and a monospace
preview showing labelled lines ("إصدار التطبيق: 1.0.0", "النوع: demo",
"الأحداث حسب الفئة:" followed by each family/count) - a deliberately
LTR-forced block despite the Arabic labels inside it, which is correct
for text meant to be pasted verbatim into a support ticket rather than
read as a bidi-optimized UI element.

Clicking "نسخ الملخص" (Copy summary) surfaced "تعذّر النسخ" (Could not
copy) - not a bug: `navigator.clipboard.writeText` in this sandboxed
browser-automation context genuinely rejects with "Write permission
denied" (confirmed directly via a debugging script:
`navigator.clipboard.writeText("test")` throws the same error outside
the app entirely). This is the exact failure path the component tests
already proved handled correctly, now also confirmed against a real
browser's actual security policy rather than only a mock - the error is
caught, shown plainly, and nothing crashes or logs to the console.
English mode re-checked separately: same card, same body text, correctly
translated. Console clean throughout both languages.

## 6. What remains in Phase 5

Incidents master/detail with search/sort/filter; Rules bilingual
catalogue improvements; Settings regrouped into six sections with a
sticky save/discard bar and unsaved-changes guard - two of that
spec's six groups ("local AI & model management", "updates & release
channel") reference features that do not exist yet (Phase 6 has not
started; there is no update-checking UI today), so building hollow
settings for them now would be exactly the kind of half-finished
implementation this project's own discipline rules against. Deferred
until the features they configure actually exist, rather than stubbed.
No new ADR needed for this slice - a pure frontend addition using
information already computed for display.
